WEBVTT

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:08.365
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake: add a column to blocks, gas used percent, a double.

00:00:15.384 --> 00:00:17.770
It is instant: no file is rewritten.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:19.973
Old rows simply have no value.

00:00:20.253 --> 00:00:25.132
Fill it for one day, the second of November: gas used divided by the gas limit.

00:00:26.082 --> 00:00:29.819
Miner is an old name; today the field means the fee recipient.

00:00:30.099 --> 00:00:30.803
Rename it.

00:00:31.753 --> 00:00:33.859
And make number required: not null.

00:00:35.364 --> 00:00:39.389
Query the new shape: number, fee recipient, and gas used percent.

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:43.749
Now time travel to version eleven, before these changes.

00:00:44.029 --> 00:00:45.910
The column is still called miner there.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.017
Each snapshot keeps its own schema.

00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:56.170
In Data Lake, open the blocks table and its Schema tab: fee recipient in place of miner, and the new column at the end.

00:01:05.246 --> 00:01:07.324
How does this work without touching files?

00:01:07.604 --> 00:01:10.101
Every column has an id in the catalog.

00:01:10.381 --> 00:01:15.482
A rename changes the name, not the id, so old files still map to the right column.

00:01:16.432 --> 00:01:23.328
You can add, drop and rename columns, promote types the safe way, like integer to big integer, and add not null.

00:01:23.608 --> 00:01:25.604
And every version keeps its own schema.

00:01:26.554 --> 00:01:31.818
Save the script as 3 underscore 3 schema evolution dot sequel, commit, and push.

00:01:54.903 --> 00:02:00.909
To recap: we added a column, renamed one, and added a constraint, without rewriting a single file.

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:03.467
And time travel still sees the old schema.

00:02:03.747 --> 00:02:06.852
Next: asking the lake what changed between two versions.
